"""
This is building off of search_and_destroy.
Why another class as opposed to upgrading the existing?
    Because this class is going to be designed for many many criteria filters (i have 40+ now)
For a small amount, say sub 10; search_and_destroy class would likely be the better option,
but for so many critera's better to not use the Prosper API to do my filtering, but submit a broad request to Prosper API.
Grab that and just use internal code to identify notes to invest in.
This will be much faster for larger criteria sets.
    Why?
        Because with search_and_destroy it multithreads the listing API request by filter.
        Once this gets to a very large amount my iddy bitty VM can't submit more than ~ 6 request per second anyway.
        Better to have 10 or less Prosper API requests (still multithreaded) and have my internal python go through the result and submit order requests.
        My internal filter code will also be multithreaded up to an amount that wont be too slow vs just looping. (thinking max 10) TEST THIS to see what is faster since its just python code if's essenitally.
        Current plan is to pull the json result from Prosper and modify it (add to it) as more results are found.
        The json result will be a mutli-thred global object.
        Will keep a tracking dict that pops the listing_number json result once all filters have checked it.
        Add code that submits additional orders if another filter finds it w/ a larger bid_amt to meet that bid_amt.
            This is needed as I have a ton of overlap w/ so many filters.
        Will track all filters that find a listing.
        Will need to add a filters_v2 as well to account for the change as i wont be using Prosper API strictly for filtering.
        CREATE TESTS THIS TIME

"""


